#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275182 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee15db10-b92c-5f48-8983-e602068c7e08" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Horizontal Asymptote of a Rational Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "91924e9c-268a-549b-acdb-3bcd1759bd84" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275184}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "91924e9c-268a-549b-acdb-3bcd1759bd84" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275184} :answer-field/id #uuid "94f42605-5fb1-5527-bedd-e379c95369fe" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275183}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e6f0ac3c-98f9-5ddd-b191-1756531965cc" :question/math-academy-id "q-339641" :question/problem "What is the horizontal asymptote of $y=f(x)$ for $x>0$ with $f(x)=\\frac{|3-8x|}{4x+1}?$\n\nThe horizontal asymptote is $y=$\n\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "For large positive values of $x,$ we have $|3-8x|=8x-3.$ Consequently, for $x\\to \\infty ,$ we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{|3-8x|}{4x+1}={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{8x-3}{4x+1}.\n$$\n\nThe horizontal asymptote for $x>0$ is $y={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is $x.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{8x-3}{4x+1} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{8x}{x}-\\frac{3}{x})}{(\\frac{4x}{x}+\\frac{1}{x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(8-\\frac{3}{x})}{(4+\\frac{1}{x})} \\\\  & =\\frac{8-0}{4+0} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the horizontal asymptote is $y=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275192 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee15db10-b92c-5f48-8983-e602068c7e08" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Horizontal Asymptote of a Rational Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bc2d79df-16bd-54f3-90e0-fae80e320a4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275194} {:answer/id #uuid "c745edd4-05d9-53f7-abab-2c20c7db0f09" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No horizontal asymptote" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275195} {:answer/id #uuid "852e1584-cf82-51dd-b11f-0e961c5afcb2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275196} {:answer/id #uuid "c4084f72-1b9c-5365-8522-d0b138dc08fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{7}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275197} {:answer/id #uuid "8b3bc636-484d-5b85-96b3-0d5c42d00ece" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275198}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c4084f72-1b9c-5365-8522-d0b138dc08fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{7}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275197} :answer-field/id #uuid "35ca9071-6662-5afe-ba8d-2d66e1d227d0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275193}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "89041eed-f9ba-5e78-85a0-a5586670c312" :question/math-academy-id "q-35831" :question/problem "Find the horizontal asymptote of the function $f(x)=\\frac{14{x}^{3}}{6{x}^{3}+1}.$" :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote is $y={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{3}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{14{x}^{3}}{6{x}^{3}+1} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{14{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}})}{(\\frac{6{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{14}{(6+\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{14}{6+0} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the horizontal asymptote of $f(x)$ is $y=\\frac{7}{3}.$"}]}
